{#- layout.njk: the shared page shell for Home and About.

    Each page names this file in its front matter ("layout: layout.njk").
    Eleventy builds the page's own content first, then puts it into this
    file where {{ content }} is.

    Values in double curly braces come from the page's front matter:
    title, description and bodyClass. "page" is a variable Eleventy adds
    to every page; page.url is the page's address ("/" for Home,
    "/about.html" for About).

    This is a Nunjucks comment: it isn't copied into the HTML. The dash
    at its start and end also removes the whitespace next to it, so the
    built file starts right at the doctype. -#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
  <meta name="author" content="James Villanueva">
  <title>{{ title }}</title>
  {#- Relative paths: both pages are in the top level of _site, next to
      styles.css and the images folder. #}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
  <link rel="icon" href="images/headshot.jpg" type="image/jpeg">
</head>
{#- The two pages use different classes from styles.css: "block-cards"
    on Home and "about-page" on About. #}
<body class="{{ bodyClass }}">
  <header>
    <p><a href="index.html">James Villanueva</a></p>
    {#- Only the two pages in this exercise. The portfolio's other nav
        links are left out because those pages aren't in _site, so the
        links would be broken. No theme toggle: this exercise has no
        JavaScript.
        aria-current="page" marks the link to the page you're on. The
        if tag adds it only on the matching page. #}
    <nav aria-label="Main">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="index.html"{% if page.url == "/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="about.html"{% if page.url == "/about.html" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>About</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </header>
  {#- The page's own content goes inside <main>. "| safe" tells Nunjucks
      the content is HTML we wrote ourselves, so it's inserted as HTML
      instead of being escaped into visible text like "&lt;h1&gt;". #}

  <main>
{{ content | safe }}
  </main>
  {#- The shared footer, from _includes/footer.njk. Change the footer in
      that one file, rebuild, and both pages update. #}
  {% include "footer.njk" %}
</body>
</html>
